          "description": {
            "en": "Prints n! up to 20!, finds the largest factorial that fits in a 64-bit long, and shows 21! silently overflowing.",
            "es": "Imprime n! hasta 20!, encuentra el factorial más grande que cabe en un long de 64 bits y muestra cómo 21! se desborda en silencio."
          },
          "explanation": {
            "en": "n! = n · (n-1) · ... · 2 · 1 counts the ways to arrange n distinct items in a row, with 0! = 1 because there is exactly one way to arrange nothing. It satisfies the recursive rule n! = n · (n-1)!.\n\n`factorial(n)` multiplies 2..n in a loop. The table shows how fast it grows: 10! is already 3,628,800 and 20! has 19 digits.\n\nThe search for the largest n with n! ≤ 2^63 - 1 never overflows because it tests `f <= MAX / (n + 1)` before multiplying, instead of multiplying first and checking afterwards. It stops at 20.\n\nThe last three lines compare the exact 21! = 51090942171709440000 with what a 64-bit signed integer stores: -4249290049419214848. Java `long` wraps around without any error, which is why it is dangerous. Each language reaches that value differently:\n- Java computes `factorial(20) * 21` and wraps;\n- C++ multiplies as unsigned, because signed overflow is undefined behavior;\n- Python and JavaScript keep exact integers (`BigInt` in JS) and then keep only the low 64 bits.\n\nPitfall: never compute C(n, k) as n!/(k!(n-k)!) with 64-bit integers for n > 20; the intermediate factorial overflows even when the answer is small.",
            "es": "n! = n · (n-1) · ... · 2 · 1 cuenta las formas de ordenar n objetos distintos en fila, con 0! = 1 porque hay exactamente una forma de ordenar nada. Cumple la regla recursiva n! = n · (n-1)!.\n\n`factorial(n)` multiplica 2..n en un ciclo. La tabla muestra lo rápido que crece: 10! ya es 3,628,800 y 20! tiene 19 dígitos.\n\nLa búsqueda del mayor n con n! ≤ 2^63 - 1 nunca se desborda porque prueba `f <= MAX / (n + 1)` antes de multiplicar, en lugar de multiplicar primero y verificar después. Se detiene en 20.\n\nLas últimas tres líneas comparan el valor exacto 21! = 51090942171709440000 con lo que guarda un entero con signo de 64 bits: -4249290049419214848. El `long` de Java da la vuelta sin ningún error, y por eso es peligroso. Cada lenguaje llega a ese valor de forma distinta:\n- Java calcula `factorial(20) * 21` y da la vuelta;\n- C++ multiplica sin signo, porque el desbordamiento con signo es comportamiento indefinido;\n- Python y JavaScript mantienen enteros exactos (`BigInt` en JS) y luego conservan solo los 64 bits bajos.\n\nError común: nunca calcules C(n, k) como n!/(k!(n-k)!) con enteros de 64 bits para n > 20; el factorial intermedio se desborda aunque la respuesta sea pequeña."
          },
          "code": {
            "java": "import java.math.BigInteger;\n\n// n! grows so fast that 21! no longer fits in a 64-bit long\npublic class FactorialOverflow {\n    static long factorial(int n) {\n        long ans = 1; // 0! = 1: there is exactly one way to arrange nothing\n        for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) ans *= i;\n        return ans;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        for (int n : new int[] {0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 10, 15, 20}) {\n            System.out.printf(\"%2d! = %d%n\", n, factorial(n));\n        }\n        System.out.println(\"recursive rule: 20! == 20 * 19! -> \" + (factorial(20) == 20 * factorial(19)));\n\n        // Find the largest n with n! <= Long.MAX_VALUE without ever overflowing\n        long f = 1;\n        int n = 0;\n        while (f <= Long.MAX_VALUE / (n + 1)) {\n            n++;\n            f *= n;\n        }\n        System.out.println(\"largest n with n! <= 2^63-1: \" + n);\n\n        BigInteger exact = BigInteger.valueOf(factorial(20)).multiply(BigInteger.valueOf(21));\n        long wrapped = factorial(20) * 21; // Java long arithmetic wraps around silently\n        System.out.println(\"21! exactly          = \" + exact);\n        System.out.println(\"long max (2^63-1)    = \" + Long.MAX_VALUE);\n        System.out.println(\"21! in a 64-bit long = \" + wrapped + \" (overflowed, no error raised)\");\n    }\n}\n",
            "cpp": "// n! grows so fast that 21! no longer fits in a 64-bit long long\n#include <cstdint>\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <limits>\n#include <string>\n\nlong long factorial(int n) {\n    long long ans = 1;  // 0! = 1: there is exactly one way to arrange nothing\n    for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) ans *= i;\n    return ans;\n}\n\n// The standard library cannot print a 128-bit integer, so convert digit by digit\nstd::string toString(unsigned __int128 x) {\n    std::string s;\n    do {\n        s.insert(s.begin(), static_cast<char>('0' + static_cast<int>(x % 10)));\n        x /= 10;\n    } while (x > 0);\n    return s;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    for (int n : {0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 10, 15, 20}) std::printf(\"%2d! = %lld\\n\", n, factorial(n));\n    std::printf(\"recursive rule: 20! == 20 * 19! -> %s\\n\", factorial(20) == 20 * factorial(19) ? \"true\" : \"false\");\n\n    // Find the largest n with n! <= LLONG_MAX without ever overflowing\n    const long long maxValue = std::numeric_limits<long long>::max();\n    long long f = 1;\n    int n = 0;\n    while (f <= maxValue / (n + 1)) {\n        n++;\n        f *= n;\n    }\n    std::printf(\"largest n with n! <= 2^63-1: %d\\n\", n);\n\n    unsigned __int128 exact = static_cast<unsigned __int128>(factorial(20)) * 21;\n    // Signed overflow is undefined behavior in C++, so wrap through unsigned to show what hardware does\n    auto wrapped = static_cast<long long>(static_cast<std::uint64_t>(factorial(20)) * 21u);\n    std::printf(\"21! exactly          = %s\\n\", toString(exact).c_str());\n    std::printf(\"long max (2^63-1)    = %lld\\n\", maxValue);\n    std::printf(\"21! in a 64-bit long = %lld (overflowed, no error raised)\\n\", wrapped);\n}\n",
            "python": "# n! grows so fast that 21! no longer fits in a 64-bit integer (Python ints never overflow)\nLONG_MAX = 2**63 - 1\n\n\ndef factorial(n: int) -> int:\n    ans = 1  # 0! = 1: there is exactly one way to arrange nothing\n    for i in range(2, n + 1):\n        ans *= i\n    return ans\n\n\ndef to_int64(x: int) -> int:\n    # Keep only the low 64 bits and read them as two's complement, like Java's long\n    return (x + 2**63) % 2**64 - 2**63\n\n\nfor n in (0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 10, 15, 20):\n    print(f\"{n:2d}! = {factorial(n)}\")\nprint(f\"recursive rule: 20! == 20 * 19! -> {str(factorial(20) == 20 * factorial(19)).lower()}\")\n\n# Find the largest n with n! <= 2^63-1\nf, n = 1, 0\nwhile f * (n + 1) <= LONG_MAX:\n    n += 1\n    f *= n\nprint(f\"largest n with n! <= 2^63-1: {n}\")\n\nprint(f\"21! exactly          = {factorial(21)}\")\nprint(f\"long max (2^63-1)    = {LONG_MAX}\")\nprint(f\"21! in a 64-bit long = {to_int64(factorial(21))} (overflowed, no error raised)\")\n",
            "javascript": "// n! grows so fast that 21! no longer fits in a 64-bit integer\n// JS numbers are exact only up to 2^53, so all factorials here use BigInt (the n suffix)\nconst LONG_MAX = 2n ** 63n - 1n;\n\nconst factorial = (n) => {\n  let ans = 1n; // 0! = 1: there is exactly one way to arrange nothing\n  for (let i = 2n; i <= BigInt(n); i++) ans *= i;\n  return ans;\n};\n\nfor (const n of [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 10, 15, 20]) {\n  console.log(`${String(n).padStart(2)}! = ${factorial(n)}`);\n}\nconsole.log(`recursive rule: 20! == 20 * 19! -> ${factorial(20) === 20n * factorial(19)}`);\n\n// Find the largest n with n! <= 2^63-1\nlet f = 1n;\nlet n = 0n;\nwhile (f * (n + 1n) <= LONG_MAX) {\n  n++;\n  f *= n;\n}\nconsole.log(`largest n with n! <= 2^63-1: ${n}`);\n\nconsole.log(`21! exactly          = ${factorial(21)}`);\nconsole.log(`long max (2^63-1)    = ${LONG_MAX}`);\n// BigInt.asIntN keeps the low 64 bits as a signed value, exactly what a Java long does\nconsole.log(`21! in a 64-bit long = ${BigInt.asIntN(64, factorial(21))} (overflowed, no error raised)`);\n"
          },
